#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106673} :activity/id #uuid "e8bbfc2d-b5f4-57c0-9027-1b66b844109b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107090 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." :tutorial/id #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3557 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b7139a7-3f19-5d72-857b-7a01a291e46b" :step/math-academy-id 20075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105620 :question/id #uuid "61bb0d60-a61e-5485-9df5-308e16dfd1aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5476"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399538}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "4921a5cc-f26d-5b7a-9865-e447eec1d53e" :step/math-academy-id 20077 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105621 :question/id #uuid "f4ff15d3-1a6e-56c0-a7c4-13f18ef1a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5477"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345122}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "64cdc019-4015-5867-b014-691bcc52330a" :step/math-academy-id 20078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105622 :question/id #uuid "6cba45d6-5a69-5b83-937c-c19368aaeea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369017}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "6e9a9912-8def-5a76-892f-0608e119d89c" :step/math-academy-id 20081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105628 :question/id #uuid "df40f37e-0d8f-5eb2-ad8a-9257440851df" :question/math-academy-id "e-5891"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393556}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "5b97f2fc-3025-5372-9dc4-19c05d084b76" :step/math-academy-id 21410 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107098 :tutorial/content "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." :tutorial/id #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3963 :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "ab53090e-96b8-5dcc-b77f-6ff038689cac" :step/math-academy-id 23514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106711}}] :activity/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106002}]}
